NNAAMMEE
     ggppttbboooott..eeffii — GPT bootcode for UFS on UEFI computers

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ggppttbboooott..eeffii is used on UEFI computers to boot from a UFS partition on a
     GPT-partitioned disk.  ggppttbboooott..eeffii is installed in the EFI System Parti‐
     tion (ESP).  For BIOS-based computers, see gptboot(8) for details.  While
     conceptually similar, the details differ.  ggppttbboooott..eeffii works only with
     UFS root file systems.  Users with ZFS partitions should use
     loader.efi(8) with bectl(8) to control what dataset is used for root.

     What UEFI computers boot is usually controlled via the mechanisms ex‐
     plained in efibootmgr(8) using loader.efi(8) or boot1.efi(8).  However,
     some setups cannot use those mechanisms.  When the users cannot rely on
     host-supplied UEFI variables or they want the contents of the media alone
     to decide root, ggppttbboooott..eeffii accomplishes these goals.

     When it starts, ggppttbboooott..eeffii first reads the GPT and determines which
     drive and partition to boot from, as described under _B_O_O_T_I_N_G, below.  If
     it does not find an eligible partition, it returns to the UEFI firmware.
     The firmware will then try the next bootable item in the UEFI Boot Man‐
     ager's list, if any, usually a different disk.

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The GPT standard allows a variable number of partitions, but ggppttbboooott..eeffii
     only boots from tables with 128 partitions or less.

PPAARRTTIITTIIOONN AATTTTRRIIBBUUTTEESS
     ggppttbboooott..eeffii checks and manages several attributes of GPT UFS partitions.
     These flags are FreeBSD specific and non-standard.

     bboooottmmee      Attempt to boot from this partition.  If more than one parti‐
                 tion has the bboooottmmee attribute set, ggppttbboooott..eeffii will attempt
                 to boot each one until successful.

     bboooottoonnccee    Attempt to boot from this partition only one time.  Setting
                 this attribute with gpart(8) automatically also sets the
                 bboooottmmee attribute.  Multiple partitions may have the bboooottoonnccee
                 and bboooottmmee attributes set.

     bboooottffaaiilleedd  The bboooottffaaiilleedd attribute marks partitions that had the
                 bboooottoonnccee attribute set, but failed to boot.  This attribute
                 is managed by the system.  See _B_O_O_T_I_N_G and _P_O_S_T_-_B_O_O_T _A_C_T_I_O_N_S
                 below for details.

UUSSAAGGEE
     For normal usage, the user does not have to set or manage any of the par‐
     tition attributes.  ggppttbboooott..eeffii will boot from the first UFS partition
     found on the device.

     The bboooottoonnccee attribute can be used for testing an upgraded operating sys‐
     tem on an already-working computer.  The existing system partition is
     left untouched, and the new version of the operating system to be tested
     is installed on another partition.  The bboooottoonnccee attribute is set on that
     new test partition.  The next boot is attempted from the test partition.
     Success or failure will be shown in the system log files.  After a suc‐
     cessful boot of the test partition, a user script can check the logs and
     change the bboooottmmee attributes so the test partition becomes the new system
     partition.  Because the bboooottoonnccee attribute is cleared after an attempted
     boot, a failed boot will not leave the system attempting to boot from a
     partition that will never succeed.  Instead, the system will boot from
     the older, known-working operating system that has not been modified.  If
     the bboooottmmee attribute is set on any partitions, booting will be attempted
     from them first.  If no partitions with bboooottmmee attributes are found,
     booting will be attempted from the first UFS partition found.

BBOOOOTTIINNGG
     ggppttbboooott..eeffii first reads the partition table.  All ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss partitions
     with only the bboooottoonnccee attribute set, indicating a failed boot, are set
     to bboooottffaaiilleedd.  ggppttbboooott..eeffii then scans through all of the ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss
     partitions.  Boot behavior depends on the combination of bboooottmmee and
     bboooottoonnccee attributes set on those partitions.

     bboooottoonnccee ++ bboooottmmee      Highest priority: booting is attempted from each
                            of the ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss partitions with both of these
                            attributes.  On each partition, the bboooottmmee attri‐
                            bute is removed and the boot attempted.

     bboooottmmee                 Middle priority: booting is attempted from each of
                            the ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss partitions with the bboooottmmee attri‐
                            bute.

     If neither bboooottoonnccee nor bboooottmmee attributes are found on any partitions,
     booting is attempted from the first ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss partition on the disk.

PPOOSSTT--BBOOOOTT AACCTTIIOONNSS
     The startup script _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._d_/_g_p_t_b_o_o_t checks the attributes of ffrreeeebbssdd--uuffss
     partitions on all GPT disks.  Partitions with the bboooottffaaiilleedd attribute
     generate a “boot from X failed” system log message.  Partitions with only
     the bboooottoonnccee attribute, indicating a partition that successfully booted,
     generate a “boot from X succeeded” system log message.  The bboooottffaaiilleedd
     attributes are cleared from all the partitions.  The bboooottoonnccee attribute
     is cleared from the partition that successfully booted.  There is nor‐
     mally only one of these.

FFIILLEESS
     /boot/gptboot.efi  bootcode binary
     /boot/efi/efi/boot/bootx64.efi
                        Default boot loader for amd64 systems.
     /boot/efi/efi/boot/bootaa64.efi
                        Default boot loader for arm64 systems.
     /boot/efi/efi/boot/bootarm.efi
                        Default boot loader for arm systems.
     /boot/efi/efi/boot/bootriscv64.efi
                        Default boot loader for riscv systems.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     ggppttbboooott..eeffii is installed in the ESP with cp(1).

     Install ggppttbboooott..eeffii into the ESP for the system.  This assumes the ESP is
     mounted in the standard _/_b_o_o_t_/_e_f_i directory.  For amd64, use the follow‐
     ing
           cp /boot/gptboot.efi /boot/efi/efi/boot/bootx64.efi
     For other systems, use the file listed in the _F_I_L_E_S section.

     Set the bboooottmmee attribute for partition 2:

           gpart set -a bootme -i 2 ada0

     Set the bboooottoonnccee attribute for partition 2, automatically also setting
     the bboooottmmee attribute:

           gpart set -a bootonce -i 2 ada0

